{
  "title": "Lebanon today · 9 September 2026",
  "paragraphs": [
    "The Lebanese Army warned today, 9 September, that continuing Israeli strikes and demolitions were obstructing its deployment under the framework agreement. It cited an earlier Israeli attack on Kfar Roummane, near Nabatieh, that had killed at least 12 people, including women and children. LBCI, citing the National News Agency, also reported an Israeli drone dropping explosives onto Mansouri during artillery shelling.",
    "President Joseph Aoun linked army deployment in Nabatieh to Lebanon's negotiating position. But an army statement reproduced by South Lebanon Watch illustrated the limits of deployment: only 60 of 450 families in Zawtar al-Gharbiyeh had been able to resettle because of Israeli destruction. Being able to enter a village does not mean being able to live there.",
    "A US State Department spokesperson, quoted by Bint Jbeil News, said another round of talks in Rome was expected soon. Meanwhile, Iran's Revolutionary Guards listed Israeli withdrawal from Lebanon alongside the release of frozen Iranian assets among their demands. No new settlement was announced in the available reports.",
    "The government's wager is understandable: a stronger army presence can reassure residents and improve Lebanon's bargaining position. The danger is that the army assumes responsibility for security without gaining the power to guarantee it. Continued Israeli attacks can then discredit the institution expected to protect residents, even as its soldiers patrol more streets. A checkpoint controls movement; it cannot prevent an airstrike.",
    "Iran's demands reveal a further constraint: support for Israeli withdrawal can be bundled with priorities Beirut does not control. Such bargaining might generate pressure on Israel, but it could also make Lebanese safety dependent on disputes over another country's money. The practical test of the next talks is whether attacks stop and families can return and stay. State authority needs habitable towns as well as deployed troops."
  ],
  "keyPoints": [
    "Army deployment cannot substitute for protection from Israeli attacks.",
    "Safe return requires homes people can inhabit.",
    "Lebanon's bargaining power remains constrained by decisions made in Israel, Washington and Tehran."
  ],
